# Build context exclusions for the repository-root production build
# (deploy/render/Dockerfile). No other image in this repository uses the root
# as its context — backend/Dockerfile uses backend/ and keeps its own
# backend/.dockerignore — so this file governs the production image alone.
#
# Two reasons a path is here: it would put something in the production image
# that has no business in production, or it would put something in a build
# context that must never leave a developer's machine.

# Never in a build context, at all.
.env
.env.*
*.env
!env.example
**/.env
**/.env.*
secrets/
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.keystore

# Not needed at runtime: the production image serves the API and the two
# workers. The mobile client is built by EAS, not by this image.
frontend/

# History and CI machinery. .git in particular would carry every secret ever
# committed and every branch, into an image that only needs one commit's files.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.gitconfig
.gitleaksignore

# Documentation, governance and reports: read by people, not by processes.
docs/
memory/
test_reports/
security/
*.md
!backend/**/*.md

# Development and qualification artefacts.
tests/
backend/tests/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
**/.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
htmlcov/
.coverage
.coverage.*
**/node_modules/
.emergent/
ci_status.json

# Compose and local tooling. Production topology is render.yaml.
docker-compose*.yml
docker-compose*.yaml

# Everything under scripts/ except the one operator the production runtime
# genuinely needs. Root scripts are developer and CI tooling; shipping them
# would widen what an authorized operator can reach inside the container for
# no operational benefit.
scripts/*
!scripts/operate_step8i_petrolatum_release.py
